## What was checked

The component and pattern indexes were read on **12 November 2026**. The Publishing frontend-template index was also checked. The inventory records direct entries, including the Home Office's named query routes; it does not count every descendant example as a separate component. The existing Publishing inventory includes 42 pages across nested examples and variations, while this top-level review records 26 direct entries. Those counts have different scopes.

Specific rules were checked against their owning guidance where they affect a decision: GOV.UK slider questions, numeric entry, grouping, choice, summary and error components, NHS cards/images, Home Office long-list guidance, W3C slider/dragging requirements, the MOJ notification badge and status model, DWP's retired key details bar and the Home Office time-display component. Entries marked **not selected** are recorded for completeness; they are not silently adopted or presented as a completed detail-page assessment.

GOV.UK brand foundations remain covered by the [brand guidance inventory](../reference/brand-guidance.json). Its detailed chapter review keeps its own date. The current brand landing structure was rechecked for this review. Supplied civica.uk artwork, Arial typography and the prohibition on official government branding remain in force.

## The slider finding

None of the eight reviewed component indexes lists a slider or range component. This does not rule out an old prototype, a community suggestion or a component outside the selected catalogues. We did find a published [USWDS range slider](https://designsystem.digital.gov/components/range-slider/), but that is from the United States and is not evidence of a GOV.UK, MOJ, NHS or Scottish Government component.

GOV.UK [Question pages](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/patterns/question-pages/#using-range-sliders) advises against using sliders for questions and requires an alternative to dragging when they are used. Home Assistant still has legitimate continuous-adjustment tasks. The [civica.uk slider component](../components/sliders.html) therefore documents a deliberate local extension: a native range with precise text entry and visible step buttons, all editing one draft. It follows the [W3C slider pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/slider/) and separates requested values from reported state.

It does not import a USWDS implementation, claim an official UK slider design, or replace every native Home Assistant slider. The [continuous-controls pattern](../home-assistant/continuous-controls.html) remains responsible for integration ranges, commits, remote updates and failures.

## Choose the pattern by meaning

| Home Assistant need | Preferred design basis | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Enter, choose or validate an ordinary value | GOV.UK fieldsets, text inputs, radios, checkboxes, selects and errors | Preserve the actual input type, label, unit and integration capability |
| Adjust a bounded setting | Local slider component plus native HA semantics | No direct slider component in the reviewed UK catalogues |
| Start an action or request a consequential change | GOV.UK buttons and MOJ confirmation principles | Request accepted is different from physical outcome |
| Show new or unread items | MOJ notification badge | Do not reuse a status tag as a notification count or badge every ordinary number |
| Summarise a device or room | NHS/card principles and GOV.UK summary content | A card is not an instruction to make the entire area clickable around nested controls |
| Find or manage many entities | GOV.UK selection primitives, MOJ filtering/table patterns and Home Office long-list guidance | Native IDs, eligibility, permissions and selection scope remain authoritative |
| Display charts | ONS chart guidance and the local chart specification | The maintained adapter covers named Energy Cartesian graphs; not every chart or flow diagram |
| Operate cameras, media, maps, voice or automation graphs | Native Home Assistant controls plus local task contracts | A publication image, video or question page is not an equivalent device interface |

GOV.UK selects are a last resort for public-facing questions, with smaller visible choices preferred. Its guidance permits an existing selection for settings. Frequently used Home Assistant settings are a controlled adaptation: retain the actual native choice and multiplicity, rather than changing every picker to the same dropdown.

The same need may appear in several source systems. Keep one local owner and document its adaptation, instead of stacking incompatible component styles. An upstream name match alone is insufficient: a task list, household to-do list and device programme progress all need different state semantics.

## Adoption and source status

**Adopted** identifies a concrete bounded implementation, such as the MOJ-style notification badge. **Adapted** means that the cited interaction principle informs a local contract with recorded changes. **No direct equivalent** identifies a full native task or control that these catalogues do not provide. It does not mean Home Assistant lacks the feature.

For individual inventory entries, **mapped reference** points to an existing local guide, while **mapped principle** points to a task-family decision. **Not selected** means no additional component is adopted in this review. **Not applicable** covers an excluded identity/domain requirement or a retired design.

Source maturity must be checked separately. MOJ distinguishes Official, To be reviewed, Experimental and Archived building blocks. Its [notification badge is Official](https://design-patterns.service.justice.gov.uk/components/notification-badge/); that status is not assigned to every indexed MOJ component, and its accessibility statements do not certify this adaptation. [DWP's key details bar is retired](https://design-system.dwp.gov.uk/components/key-details-bar); it should not be introduced as a new preferred device summary. DWP directs new work to Quick reference.

The Home Office [Time component](https://design.homeoffice.gov.uk/design-system/components?name=Time) displays a time. It is not an editable time picker. Its display preferences also do not override a Home Assistant user's chosen clock format or configured time zone.

